  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Nuclear density is given by,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mfenced><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math>, where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;is mass of proton. Clearly it&nbsp;is independent of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;and same for all nucleus.</p><p>Radius of the nucleus is given by,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msup></math>.</p>",
